            "**Examination:** good light, gloves, gauze and a mirror. **Try to scrape it** - record that it does not come off. Note **site, size, exact borders, colour (homogeneous white, speckled, mixed red and white), surface (smooth, wrinkled, nodular, verrucous, ulcerated) and above all INDURATION on palpation**, which is the sign of invasion. Examine the **whole mucosa including the floor of the mouth, ventral and lateral tongue, retromolar trigone, soft palate and oropharynx**, since field change is the rule; check **mouth opening** for submucous fibrosis; examine the **teeth and dentures for a traumatic cause**; and **palpate the cervical lymph nodes** systematically.",
            "**Bedside and simple tests:** a **KOH mount or Gram-stained smear** of a scraping - candidal hyphae would support chronic hyperplastic candidiasis; **blood glucose and HbA1c**; **haemoglobin, ferritin, B12 and folate**; and **HIV testing after counselling** where the picture suggests it.",
            "**The definitive investigation is an incisional biopsy** taken from the **most suspicious area - the red, speckled, nodular, indurated or ulcerated part, not the innocuous white centre** - with adequate depth, stained with haematoxylin and eosin and with **PAS for candidal hyphae**. The report gives the diagnosis and, crucially, the **grade of epithelial dysplasia**. Toluidine blue staining and brush cytology may guide site selection but never replace the biopsy.",
            "**A therapeutic trial has a defined place:** if candidal hyphae are found, give a **systemic antifungal (fluconazole or itraconazole) for 2-4 weeks together with complete tobacco cessation, and then re-examine**; the homogeneous plaque of chronic hyperplastic candidiasis often regresses substantially, and **any residual lesion is biopsied or re-biopsied**. A traumatic keratosis should resolve within 2 weeks of removing the sharp cusp or the ill-fitting denture - **a lesion that persists beyond 2-3 weeks after the cause is removed must be biopsied.**",
            "**Management by result:** **no dysplasia** - tobacco and areca nut cessation with counselling and pharmacotherapy, treat the candidiasis, remove local trauma, and review 3-monthly for the first year with photographs; **mild to moderate dysplasia** - as above plus close surveillance, with excision considered for a high-risk site or a speckled lesion; **severe dysplasia, carcinoma in situ or carcinoma** - refer urgently to the head and neck or maxillofacial oncology service for surgical excision (scalpel, laser or cryotherapy) and staging.",
            "**Counsel and follow up for life.** Explain that the patch is a **potentially malignant disorder**, that **stopping all tobacco and areca nut is the single most effective treatment**, and that the risk of a second lesion elsewhere in the mouth persists. Teach **oral self-examination monthly** and the symptoms that mean an immediate visit - a non-healing ulcer of over 2 weeks, a lump, bleeding, numbness, a change in colour or texture, loose teeth or difficulty swallowing. **Screening the household for tobacco use, and opportunistic oral examination of every tobacco user attending the clinic, is the family physician's contribution to oral cancer control.**",
